1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 1.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:03,450
2, Editions and Licensing.

3
00:00:03,700 --> 00:00:08,500
Before anyone installs Windows Server,
someone has to decide which edition to

4
00:00:08,750 --> 00:00:14,350
buy, how many licenses the company needs,
and how the server will be installed.

5
00:00:14,600 --> 00:00:19,000
Those choices cost real money, and some
cannot be undone later.

6
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:25,250
In this video you will learn how Microsoft
licenses Windows Server 2025, how to pick

7
00:00:25,500 --> 00:00:29,900
an edition, and how to check that a server
is activated.

8
00:00:30,150 --> 00:00:31,750
This supports objectives 1.

9
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:33,200
a and 1.

10
00:00:33,450 --> 00:00:37,050
b, and the Server+ exam topics on server
administration.

11
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:38,900
Here is the plan.

12
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:42,750
First, you will compare the Windows Server
2025 editions.

13
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:48,600
Next, you will count the core licenses a
server needs, which is simple arithmetic

14
00:00:48,850 --> 00:00:50,850
once you know the rules.

15
00:00:51,100 --> 00:00:55,900
Then you will compare what Standard and
Datacenter allow with virtual machines,

16
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:59,750
and choose between user and device Client
Access Licenses.

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,800
You will also choose an installation
option.

18
00:01:03,050 --> 00:01:09,050
Finally, you will see how Windows is
activated, and how to check it in Settings

19
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:11,300
and from the command line.

20
00:01:11,550 --> 00:01:15,950
Think of four choices you make before you
build a server.

21
00:01:16,200 --> 00:01:21,000
The edition decides features and how many
virtual machines you may run.

22
00:01:21,250 --> 00:01:24,450
Licensing decides how many licenses you
must own.

23
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:28,300
The installation option decides whether
you get a desktop.

24
00:01:28,550 --> 00:01:32,150
And activation proves the copy uses a
valid key.

25
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:34,000
Now the important part.

26
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:35,850
Licensing is legal compliance.

27
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:41,700
Windows Server runs even if you bought too
few licenses, but your organization is

28
00:01:41,950 --> 00:01:43,550
still breaking its agreement.

29
00:01:43,800 --> 00:01:47,800
Prices and terms change, so always check
Microsoft's current pages.

30
00:01:48,050 --> 00:01:51,250
Windows Server 2025 has four editions to
know.

31
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:55,500
Standard is for physical servers or a few
virtual machines.

32
00:01:55,750 --> 00:02:00,950
Datacenter is for hosts with many virtual
machines, and adds features like Storage

33
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:02,400
Spaces Direct.

34
00:02:02,650 --> 00:02:08,250
Datacenter Azure Edition runs only as a
virtual machine in Azure or Azure Local,

35
00:02:08,500 --> 00:02:10,900
with hotpatching and Azure Extended
Networking.

36
00:02:11,150 --> 00:02:17,550
Essentials is for small businesses with up
to 25 users and 50 devices, sold only with

37
00:02:17,800 --> 00:02:20,200
new single-socket servers from hardware
makers.

38
00:02:20,450 --> 00:02:23,650
Our NetLab pod runs Datacenter on every
server.

39
00:02:23,900 --> 00:02:27,100
This table shows the differences that
matter most.

40
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:31,750
Standard allows two Windows Server virtual
machines per license; Datacenter allows

41
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,200
unlimited.

42
00:02:33,450 --> 00:02:35,850
Storage Spaces Direct is Datacenter only.

43
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:41,300
Storage Replica works in Standard, but
only with one partnership and a single

44
00:02:41,550 --> 00:02:43,950
volume of up to 2 terabytes.

45
00:02:44,200 --> 00:02:47,800
Network Controller, the heart of
software-defined networking, is Datacenter

46
00:02:48,050 --> 00:02:49,250
only.

47
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:53,900
Hotpatching is a paid Azure Arc service
for Standard and Datacenter.

48
00:02:54,150 --> 00:02:58,150
And only Datacenter hosts can activate
their VMs with AVMA.

49
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:02,400
Everyday roles, like DNS and Hyper-V, are
in every edition.

50
00:03:02,650 --> 00:03:07,050
Standard and Datacenter are licensed by
processor core, with four rules.

51
00:03:07,300 --> 00:03:09,300
One, license every physical core.

52
00:03:09,550 --> 00:03:13,150
Two, every processor counts as at least 8
cores.

53
00:03:13,400 --> 00:03:17,000
Three, every server needs at least 16 core
licenses.

54
00:03:17,250 --> 00:03:20,450
Four, licenses come in 2-core and 16-core
packs.

55
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:22,300
Look at the example.

56
00:03:22,550 --> 00:03:26,550
Two processors with 6 cores each is 12
real cores.

57
00:03:26,800 --> 00:03:32,000
But each processor counts as 8, so the
server needs 16 core licenses.

58
00:03:32,250 --> 00:03:36,250
We count physical cores, not
hyper-threads, and CALs are bought

59
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:37,700
separately.

60
00:03:37,950 --> 00:03:41,550
Here is a method you can use every time.

61
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:46,200
For each processor, take its cores or 8,
whichever is larger.

62
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:47,650
Add them up.

63
00:03:47,900 --> 00:03:51,100
If the total is under 16, use 16.

64
00:03:51,350 --> 00:03:54,550
Example A has one processor with 4 cores.

65
00:03:54,800 --> 00:03:58,800
It counts as 8, which is under 16, so 16.

66
00:03:59,050 --> 00:04:03,450
Example B has two processors with 12 cores
each, so 24.

67
00:04:03,700 --> 00:04:05,300
Example C is tricky.

68
00:04:05,550 --> 00:04:12,350
Four processors with 4 cores each is only
16 real cores, but each counts as 8, so

69
00:04:12,600 --> 00:04:13,800
you need 32.

70
00:04:14,050 --> 00:04:15,250
Now, virtual machines.

71
00:04:15,500 --> 00:04:21,100
When you license all the cores with
Standard, you may run two Windows Server

72
00:04:21,350 --> 00:04:22,550
virtual machines.

73
00:04:22,800 --> 00:04:24,000
Want two more?

74
00:04:24,250 --> 00:04:26,250
License all the cores again.

75
00:04:26,500 --> 00:04:28,100
This is called stacking.

76
00:04:28,350 --> 00:04:35,150
So a 16-core host needs 16 licenses for
two VMs, 32 for four, and 48 for six.

77
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:41,400
With Datacenter, license the cores once
and run as many VMs as the host can

78
00:04:41,650 --> 00:04:42,850
handle.

79
00:04:43,100 --> 00:04:47,100
The host itself is included if it only
runs Hyper-V.

80
00:04:47,350 --> 00:04:49,750
And always license for the peak.

81
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
Core licenses cover the server.

82
00:04:52,250 --> 00:04:56,650
Client Access Licenses, or CALs, cover who
or what uses it.

83
00:04:56,900 --> 00:05:00,500
A User CAL covers one person on any
device.

84
00:05:00,750 --> 00:05:06,750
Ana uses a laptop, a phone and a home PC,
and needs one User CAL.

85
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,600
A Device CAL covers one device, whoever
uses it.

86
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:16,450
Three nurses on different shifts share one
PC, so one Device CAL covers them.

87
00:05:16,700 --> 00:05:19,900
Count both ways and buy the smaller
number.

88
00:05:20,150 --> 00:05:22,550
You can also mix both types.

89
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:25,200
Remote Desktop needs extra RDS CALs.

90
00:05:25,450 --> 00:05:27,450
Windows Server 2025 adds Pay-as-you-go.

91
00:05:27,700 --> 00:05:33,700
Instead of buying licenses up front, you
connect the server to Azure with the Azure

92
00:05:33,950 --> 00:05:39,950
Arc agent, and your Azure subscription is
billed per core while it is turned on.

93
00:05:40,200 --> 00:05:46,200
Standard and Datacenter cost the same this
way, and you need no CALs for standard

94
00:05:46,450 --> 00:05:49,250
use, though RDS CALs are still needed.

95
00:05:49,500 --> 00:05:52,300
You get no extra virtual machine rights.

96
00:05:52,550 --> 00:05:56,150
Billing continues even when the server is
shut down.

97
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:00,400
Our pod has no internet, so this is a
concept.

98
00:06:00,650 --> 00:06:03,050
Every edition has two installation
options.

99
00:06:03,300 --> 00:06:07,700
Desktop Experience gives you the full
graphical interface and every tool.

100
00:06:07,950 --> 00:06:09,950
Server Core has no desktop.

101
00:06:10,200 --> 00:06:15,800
Server Manager, the MMC consoles and File
Explorer are not there, so you use

102
00:06:16,050 --> 00:06:18,050
SConfig, PowerShell or remote tools.

103
00:06:18,300 --> 00:06:24,300
In return, Core has a much smaller attack
surface, is about 4 gigabytes smaller on

104
00:06:24,550 --> 00:06:27,350
disk, and has fewer parts to patch.

105
00:06:27,600 --> 00:06:28,800
Microsoft recommends it.

106
00:06:29,050 --> 00:06:33,450
The catch: in Windows Server 2025 you
cannot switch after installation.

107
00:06:33,700 --> 00:06:36,500
Changing your mind means a clean install.

108
00:06:36,750 --> 00:06:38,750
Activation happens in several ways.

109
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:43,800
A retail key activates one copy, and OEM
servers often arrive activated.

110
00:06:44,050 --> 00:06:49,250
A Multiple Activation Key activates many
servers, each once, online or by phone.

111
00:06:49,500 --> 00:06:55,100
With KMS, a server on your network
activates others once at least five ask,

112
00:06:55,350 --> 00:06:57,750
and they renew every 180 days.

113
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:03,200
AVMA lets VMs on an activated Datacenter
Hyper-V host activate with no internet.

114
00:07:03,450 --> 00:07:09,850
The evaluation edition runs 180 days, and
you can convert it with DISM and a key.

115
00:07:10,100 --> 00:07:13,700
In our pod, the instructor already
activated the images.

116
00:07:13,950 --> 00:07:18,750
On a server with Desktop Experience, the
quickest check is in Settings.

117
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:21,400
Select Start, Settings, System, then
Activation.

118
00:07:21,650 --> 00:07:27,250
At the top you see the edition, such as
Windows Server 2025 Datacenter, and

119
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:29,900
whether it is an evaluation copy.

120
00:07:30,150 --> 00:07:31,750
Next is Activation state.

121
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:33,200
Active means activated.

122
00:07:33,450 --> 00:07:40,250
Below that, Change product key lets you
enter a new key, which we will not do in

123
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:41,700
the pod.

124
00:07:41,950 --> 00:07:47,550
This is a labelled diagram, not a
screenshot, so your screen may look a

125
00:07:47,800 --> 00:07:49,000
little different.

126
00:07:49,250 --> 00:07:53,650
Here is the command-line way, which also
works on Server Core.

127
00:07:53,900 --> 00:07:57,100
slmgr with the dli option shows license
information.

128
00:07:57,350 --> 00:08:03,750
On its own it opens a Windows Script Host
box; through cscript it prints in the

129
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:05,200
console.

130
00:08:05,450 --> 00:08:09,850
License Status: Licensed means activated,
and the description shows the channel.

131
00:08:10,100 --> 00:08:14,100
DISM with Get-CurrentEdition, run as
administrator, shows the edition name;

132
00:08:14,350 --> 00:08:17,550
Eval in the name means an evaluation copy.

133
00:08:17,800 --> 00:08:22,600
In PowerShell, a LicenseStatus of 1 means
Licensed, and 0 means Unlicensed.

134
00:08:22,850 --> 00:08:24,450
Your values will differ.

135
00:08:24,700 --> 00:08:26,300
Let's put it together.

136
00:08:26,550 --> 00:08:32,550
First, a small business with up to 25
users and 50 devices, buying a new

137
00:08:32,800 --> 00:08:34,000
single-socket server?

138
00:08:34,250 --> 00:08:35,450
Essentials may fit.

139
00:08:35,700 --> 00:08:40,500
Second, will it run as a VM in Azure or
Azure Local?

140
00:08:40,750 --> 00:08:41,950
Consider Azure Edition.

141
00:08:42,200 --> 00:08:46,200
Third, do you need Storage Spaces Direct
or Network Controller?

142
00:08:46,450 --> 00:08:47,650
Then Datacenter.

143
00:08:47,900 --> 00:08:50,300
Otherwise, count the VMs at peak.

144
00:08:50,550 --> 00:08:52,150
Zero to two: Standard.

145
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:54,400
Three to ten: price both.

146
00:08:54,650 --> 00:08:57,850
Eleven or more, or a changing number:
Datacenter.

147
00:08:58,100 --> 00:09:02,900
Standard needs a full set of core licenses
for every two VMs.

148
00:09:03,150 --> 00:09:04,750
Four mistakes to avoid.

149
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:07,800
First, licensing only the cores you use.

150
00:09:08,050 --> 00:09:13,650
License every physical core, with at least
8 per processor and 16 per server.

151
00:09:13,900 --> 00:09:17,500
Second, thinking Standard gives two VMs
per 2-core pack.

152
00:09:17,750 --> 00:09:20,950
It is two VMs per fully licensed server.

153
00:09:21,200 --> 00:09:26,800
Third, installing Desktop Experience to be
safe and planning to remove the GUI later.

154
00:09:27,050 --> 00:09:29,450
You cannot convert, so decide first.

155
00:09:29,700 --> 00:09:32,100
Fourth, thinking activated means fully
licensed.

156
00:09:32,350 --> 00:09:34,350
Activation only checks the key.

157
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:38,200
Cores, CALs and VM rights are still your
job.

158
00:09:38,450 --> 00:09:40,050
Time to check yourself.

159
00:09:40,300 --> 00:09:43,100
Pause the video and answer four questions.

160
00:09:43,350 --> 00:09:47,350
One, a server has two processors with 6
cores each.

161
00:09:47,600 --> 00:09:49,200
How many core licenses?

162
00:09:49,450 --> 00:09:54,250
Two, a 16-core host will run five Windows
Server VMs on Standard.

163
00:09:54,500 --> 00:09:56,500
How many Standard core licenses?

164
00:09:56,750 --> 00:10:02,350
Three, 40 staff each use a laptop and a
phone to reach one server.

165
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:05,400
User or device CALs, and how many?

166
00:10:05,650 --> 00:10:08,050
Four, a test server shows
ServerDatacenterEval.

167
00:10:08,300 --> 00:10:12,700
What happens after 180 days, and how do
you keep it?

168
00:10:12,950 --> 00:10:14,150
Let's check.

169
00:10:14,400 --> 00:10:16,000
One: 16 core licenses.

170
00:10:16,250 --> 00:10:20,650
Each processor counts as 8, and 8 plus 8
is 16.

171
00:10:20,900 --> 00:10:22,500
Two: 48 core licenses.

172
00:10:22,750 --> 00:10:29,550
Each full set of 16 allows two VMs, so
five VMs need three sets, which allows up

173
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:31,000
to six.

174
00:10:31,250 --> 00:10:32,850
Three: 40 User CALs.

175
00:10:33,100 --> 00:10:36,700
Device CALs would need 80, so pick the
smaller.

176
00:10:36,950 --> 00:10:38,550
Four: the evaluation expires.

177
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:43,600
Windows warns you, then shuts itself down
automatically and repeatedly until you

178
00:10:43,850 --> 00:10:46,250
convert it to a licensed edition.

179
00:10:46,500 --> 00:10:50,500
So convert it first, with DISM and a
retail key.

180
00:10:50,750 --> 00:10:53,550
An evaluation domain controller cannot be
converted.

181
00:10:53,800 --> 00:10:56,200
Let's look at a real server.

182
00:10:56,450 --> 00:11:00,450
I am signed in at the SRV01 console as
Administrator.

183
00:11:00,700 --> 00:11:04,700
I open Windows PowerShell and type slmgr
space slash dli.

184
00:11:04,950 --> 00:11:10,150
A Windows Script Host box shows the
edition, the channel, the last five

185
00:11:10,400 --> 00:11:13,600
characters of the key, and the license
status.

186
00:11:13,850 --> 00:11:17,450
Your values may differ, but you should see
Licensed.

187
00:11:17,700 --> 00:11:18,900
I select OK.

188
00:11:19,150 --> 00:11:20,750
Next I type winver.

189
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,800
About Windows shows the version and build.

190
00:11:24,050 --> 00:11:28,450
Finally, I open Settings, System,
Activation, and read the edition and

191
00:11:28,700 --> 00:11:29,900
activation state.

192
00:11:30,150 --> 00:11:31,350
To sum up.

193
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:36,000
Standard allows two VMs per fully licensed
server; Datacenter allows unlimited.

194
00:11:36,250 --> 00:11:41,450
License every physical core, at least 8
per processor and 16 per server.

195
00:11:41,700 --> 00:11:45,700
Buy user or device CALs, whichever you
need fewer of.

196
00:11:45,950 --> 00:11:49,550
Server Core is recommended, and you cannot
switch later.

197
00:11:49,800 --> 00:11:53,800
And licensing is legal compliance, so
check Microsoft's current terms.

198
00:11:54,050 --> 00:11:55,250
Lab 1.

199
00:11:55,500 --> 00:11:59,100
2 is a 40-minute scenario lab with no pod.

200
00:11:59,350 --> 00:12:04,150
You will count licenses, compare editions,
choose CALs and read activation output.

201
00:12:04,400 --> 00:12:06,400
See you in Topic 1.

202
00:12:06,650 --> 00:12:07,850
3.
